name: control flow
boss: TheFork
flavor: Every branch you don't take, I take.
syntax: arith, *
brief: Four shapes cover nearly everything you will write: if for while case.
brief:
brief: if takes a COMMAND, not an expression -- if [ -f x ] runs the command [
brief: and branches on its exit status. Anything with an exit status works
brief: there, which is why if grep -q ... is so common.
brief:
brief: for walks a list of words. while repeats until a command fails. The
brief: read loop -- while read -r line -- is how you process a file line by
brief: line without loading it all into memory. seq prints a list of numbers
brief: when you need one to walk.
brief:
brief: Inside a loop, break leaves it early and continue skips to the next
brief: round without finishing this one.
brief:
brief: $((expr)) does arithmetic -- i=$((i + 1)) is the closest thing sh has
brief: to i++. IFS=, read splits a line on commas instead of whitespace, just
brief: for that one read.
---
q: %show% exists only if @txt@ is actually there
a: if [ -f @txt@ ]; then echo exists; fi
out: ^exists$
ref: sh
teach: sh
hint: if runs the command after it and branches on that command's exit status
---
q: %show% yes if @log@ mentions @needle@ per grep, otherwise no
a: if grep -q @needle@ @log@; then echo yes; else echo no; fi
cmp: ref
ref: grep
teach: grep -q
hint: else covers exactly the case the if branch didn't take
---
q: {loop over the words a b c printing each on its own line|use for to print a, b and c each on their own line|iterate over a b c, echoing one per line}
a: for x in a b c; do echo $x; done
cmp: ref
ref: sh
teach: sh
hint: for walks a literal list of words, one iteration per word
---
q: {loop over every .log file here and print its name|use for with a *.log glob to print each matching filename|iterate over the .log files here, echoing each name}
a: for f in *.log; do echo $f; done
cmp: ref
ref: sh
teach: sh
hint: *.log expands to the matching filenames before the loop ever runs
---
q: {loop over every .log file here printing its line count alone|use for over *.log, printing each count with no filename beside it|iterate over the .log files here, printing just each line count}, with wc
a: for f in *.log; do wc -l < $f; done
out: ^[0-9]+$
cmp: ref
ref: wc
teach: wc -l
hint: $f holds one filename per iteration, ready to feed to wc
---
q: count from 1 to 5, one number per line, with seq and for
a: for i in $(seq 1 5); do echo $i; done
cmp: ref
ref: seq
teach: seq
hint: seq 1 5 generates the words a for loop then iterates over
---
q: %show% the numbers 1 to 5 with seq alone
a: seq 1 5
cmp: ref
ref: seq
teach: seq
hint: seq alone needs no loop -- it prints the whole range itself
---
q: count up from 1 to 10, by 3
a: seq 1 3 10
cmp: ref
ref: seq
teach: seq
hint: a middle argument to seq is the step, not just start and end
---
q: %show% 1 to 5 with seq, separated by commas rather than newlines
a: seq -s, 1 5
cmp: ref
ref: seq
teach: seq -s
hint: -s sets the separator seq prints between numbers
---
q: {read @txt@ line by line, printing each line as you go|use a while read loop to print every line of @txt@|iterate over @txt@ one line at a time with read, echoing each}
a: while read -r line; do echo "$line"; done < @txt@
cmp: ref
ref: read
teach: read -r
hint: the redirect goes on the done, feeding the whole loop
---
q: {read @csv@'s lines on commas, printing the first field|split @csv@ on commas with read, printing the first field|iterate over @csv@ with read, splitting commas, first field}
a: while IFS=, read -r a b c d; do echo "$a"; done < @csv@
out: ^gateway$
ref: read
teach: read -r
hint: IFS set just for the read splits the line into the named fields
---
q: {count your retries from 0 up to 2, printing each one|track attempt number 0 through 2 with a while loop, printing each|print 0, 1, 2 -- like counting attempts before giving up}
a: i=0; while [ $i -lt 3 ]; do echo $i; i=$((i + 1)); done
cmp: ref
ref: sh
teach: sh
hint: $((...)) does the arithmetic; there's no ++ in POSIX sh
---
q: {check @var@ against @word@ with case, printing hit on a match|route on the value of @var@ using case, printing hit when it's @word@|match @var@ against @word@, printing hit only when it hits}
a: @var@=@word@; case $@var@ in @word@) echo hit ;; *) echo miss ;; esac
cmp: ref
ref: sh
teach: sh
hint: each pattern ends with ) and each branch ends with ;;
---
q: {f is app.log -- print log when the name ends in .log|match f=app.log against *.log, printing log|set f=app.log, print log if it ends in .log}
a: f=app.log; case $f in *.log) echo log ;; *) echo other ;; esac
cmp: ref
ref: sh
teach: sh
hint: * inside a case pattern globs just like it does on the command line
---
q: {count 1 to 5 but break out the moment you hit 3|stop scanning 1 to 5 with break as soon as i reaches 3|walk through 1 to 5, using break to quit early at 3}
a: for i in $(seq 1 5); do [ $i -eq 3 ] && break; echo $i; done
cmp: ref
ref: break
teach: break
hint: break exits the loop immediately, skipping any remaining iterations
---
q: {print 1 to 5 but continue past 3|walk 1 to 5, using continue to skip just the 3|go through 1 to 5, leaving 3 out of the output with continue}
a: for i in $(seq 1 5); do [ $i -eq 3 ] && continue; echo $i; done
cmp: ref
ref: continue
teach: continue
hint: continue skips the rest of this iteration and moves to the next
---
q: {repeat until a condition holds -- count up to 3 with until|use until instead of while to count from 0 to 2|loop with until, counting up until the counter reaches 3}
a: i=0; until [ $i -ge 3 ]; do echo $i; i=$((i + 1)); done
cmp: ref
ref: sh
teach: sh
hint: until is while's mirror -- it loops as long as the test still fails
---
q: {what does this print?|call it -- what comes out?|read it: what does it print?}
predict: x="a b"; for w in $x; do echo $w; done | wc -l
a: 2
ref: sh
teach: sh
hint: unquoted, $x splits on the space, so for sees two words and loops twice
---
q: {what does this print?|call it -- what comes out?|read it: what does it print?}
predict: x="a b"; for w in "$x"; do echo $w; done | wc -l
a: 1
ref: sh
teach: sh
hint: quoted, "$x" is one word with the space still in it, so for loops just once
---
q: {what does this print?|call it -- what comes out?|read it: what does it print?}
predict: printf 'a\nb' | while read -r l; do echo "$l"; done | wc -l
a: 1
ref: sh
teach: sh
hint: the last line has no trailing newline, so read hits EOF and fails before the loop body runs for it
---
q: {what does this print?|call it -- what comes out?|read it: what does it print?}
predict: x=b; case $x in a|b) echo first;; b) echo second;; esac
a: first
ref: sh
teach: sh
hint: case stops at the first pattern that matches -- the later b) branch never gets a turn
---
q: {what does this print?|call it -- what comes out?|read it: what does it print?}
predict: echo $((7/2))
a: 3
ref: sh
teach: sh
hint: $(( )) is integer arithmetic -- it truncates instead of rounding, no floats involved
